---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="02-conditionals"
  lessonSlug="02-bmi-category"
  hook="Same elif chain — but now the technique is monotonic ordering: by going low-to-high, each branch's condition becomes a single upper bound. No and needed."
>
  <h2>The thresholds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>bmi &lt; 18.5</code> → <code>"Underweight"</code></li>
    <li><code>18.5 &lt;= bmi &lt; 25</code> → <code>"Normal"</code></li>
    <li><code>25 &lt;= bmi &lt; 30</code> → <code>"Overweight"</code></li>
    <li><code>bmi &gt;= 30</code> → <code>"Obese"</code></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The cleanest pattern</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def bmi_category(bmi):
    if bmi < 18.5:
        return "Underweight"
    elif bmi < 25:
        return "Normal"
    elif bmi < 30:
        return "Overweight"
    else:
        return "Obese"

for value in [17.0, 22.0, 27.5, 31.0, 18.5, 25.0, 30.0]:
    print(f"bmi_category({value:>5}) = {bmi_category(value)}")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Why each elif is a single comparison">
    <p>
      By the time Python reaches <code>elif bmi &lt; 25</code>, it already knows
      <code>bmi &gt;= 18.5</code> — otherwise the first branch would have
      returned. So <code>elif bmi &lt; 25</code> actually expresses
      <code>18.5 &lt;= bmi &lt; 25</code> implicitly.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Verbose alternative (also correct)</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="Defensive — survives someone reordering branches"
    code={`def bmi_category(bmi):
    if bmi < 18.5:
        return "Underweight"
    elif 18.5 <= bmi < 25:
        return "Normal"
    elif 25 <= bmi < 30:
        return "Overweight"
    elif bmi >= 30:
        return "Obese"

print(bmi_category(22))
print(bmi_category(18.5))
print(bmi_category(25))`}
  />

  <Quiz
    question="What does bmi_category(25.0) return with the LOW-TO-HIGH version?"
    choices={[
      { text: '"Normal"', explain: "Check: 25.0 < 25 is False. So we move to elif bmi < 30 — True. Returns 'Overweight'." },
      { text: '"Overweight"', correct: true, explain: "Right. 25.0 < 25 is False, 25.0 < 30 is True. The boundary belongs to the upper category." },
      { text: '"Obese"', explain: "Only when bmi >= 30. 25 is not >= 30." },
      { text: 'None', explain: "All branches are mutually exclusive and exhaustive — one must match." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>02-bmi-category.py</code>. Implement the function — the autograder
    runs 7 tests covering each category plus the three boundary values.
  </p>
</Lecture>
